  <datafield tag="545" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Timothy C. Ely (born in 1949) is an American painter, graphic artist, and bookbinder, known for creating single-copy handmade books as art objects.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="520" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">In this unique book, completely designed and bound by the artist, Ely uses techniques of bookbinding, painting, and drafting to express ideas related to alchemy.  It contains drawings and watercolors concerning an alchemical laboratory called the Hollow House. Ely expresses his concepts by drawing and painting cyphers and symbols and by devising codes that are meant to effect personal and unique translations of the veiled meanings analogous to the alchemical process.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="506" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Open for use by qualified researchers.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Signed by the artist on title page.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="563" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The artist's book is a drum leaf binding of seven folios with edge treatment echoing a motif from the seventh folio. The spine is covered in painted and gilded Japanese paper.  The boards are covered in wood, leather, and paper treated with gold and bole, pigments and resins, and with gold tooling. The leather was embossed with magnesium plates designed by the artist. Wax finalizes the work.  The book is laid into a drop spine or clamshell box with boards covered by dyed cloth. There are two printed paper labels, one on spine and one on the front cover. The box is laid out inside with colored printed paper and decorated with hand-painted abtract motives and geometrical drawings.</subfield>
  </datafield>
